        // stream error and retries on a fresh connection; the PTY harness
        // also kills the CLI mid-request in teardown. Simulate the cancel:
        // declare a body longer than what is sent, then destroy the socket.
        final socket = await Socket.connect('127.0.0.1', mock.port);
        const partial = '{"model": "test-model"';
        socket.write(
          'POST /v1/chat/completions HTTP/1.1\r\n'
          'Host: 127.0.0.1\r\n'
          'Content-Type: application/json\r\n'
          'Content-Length: ${partial.length + 512}\r\n'
          '\r\n'
          '$partial',
        );
        await socket.flush();
        await Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(milliseconds: 50));
        socket.destroy();
        // Let the server observe the EOF mid-body and run its handler.
        await Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(milliseconds: 250));
        await mock.close();
      }, errors.add);
      expect(errors, isEmpty, reason: ...);
    },
  );
```

Hmm wait — if the handler throws unhandled in RED state, does `await runZonedGuarded(...)` still complete normally? Yes — the zone body isn't interrupted; the error goes to the handler asynchronously. Good.

Reason text: reference the incident.

Test 2: response-stream abort (SlowThinkingMockServer):

```dart
  test('SlowThinkingMockServer: aborting the stream mid-response never fails the test', () async {
    final errors = <Object>[];
    await runZonedGuarded(() async {
      final mock = SlowThinkingMockServer();
      await mock.start();
      final client = HttpClient();
      final req = await client.openUrl('POST', Uri.parse('http://127.0.0.1:${mock.port}/v1/chat/completions'));
      req.add(utf8.encode('{}'));  // dart:io client — fine
      await req.close();
      // read the first chunk or two then destroy
      final res = await req.close();  // careful: close once
      ...
