  });
```

Simpler: raw Socket again — send a complete POST (Content-Length exact), wait ~400ms to receive first paced chunk, then destroy. Server: join() completes, first write lands, then 150ms later the next write hits the dead socket → SocketException → RED unhandled.

Hmm — actually wait. Does writing to a loopback socket whose peer destroyed produce an ERROR on the server, or does the write silently succeed (TCP buffers) until RST comes back? Peer `destroy()` sends RST (if unread data in receive buffer... with a clean half-close it'd be FIN). Socket.destroy() on the client sends RST when there is unconsumed received data (the first SSE chunk we never read!). We DO receive the first chunk and don't read it → receive buffer non-empty → destroy → RST → server's next write gets ECONNRESET → SocketException("Write failed")... hmm actually with RST received, dart:io server write → error event on the socket → response.write may throw synchronously or the error surfaces at close(). Either way an unhandled error in the handler zone IF uncaught — RED shows it. But careful: with SMALL writes fully absorbed by kernel buffers and NO RST (clean FIN from half-closeSocket?) — Socket.destroy() = immediate RST ("the socket is destroyed... pending writes discarded; remote gets RST if data was buffered"?). Empirically: destroy() with unread inbound data → RST. Our test client deliberately doesn't read after the first chunk → good.

Edge: if the RST doesn't materialize and writes just buffer, no server error → test 2 green-in-red-state (false pass). Acceptable: test 1 is the load-bearing RED; test 2 documents the response-abort contract. But better make test 2 also RED-verifiable NOW — let me just run the experiment to see what actually throws.

Also: `makeTempHomeForMock`... not needed here.

One more design question: should the reg test import the fixtures from `test/integration/`? Relative import from `test/integration/xxx_reg_test.dart` → `import 'fa_cli_fixtures.dart';` — same dir. But WAIT: will the default-suite include this file? dart test globs `test/**/*_test.dart`. Yes. And `--exclude-tags integration` (quality gate invocation) doesn't exclude untagged files. It runs in the default suite — 2 extra fast tests (~1s). 

